1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,000
A music video made from one prompt reached 2.1 million views, the post says. The model that made it cannot output video at all.

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:20,500
Here is the trick. Do not ask for a video. Ask for the picture at time t. Then ask nine hundred times.

3
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:32,000
The model writes a program with one special function, seek. Give it a time and it arranges everything exactly as that instant should look.

4
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:42,000
A headless browser, a browser with no window, loads the program, calls seek, and takes a screenshot. One photo per frame.

5
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:52,500
Fifteen seconds at sixty frames per second is nine hundred photos. Same time, same picture, every run.

6
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:02,000
ffmpeg glues the photos into a film and lays the audio underneath. That is the whole machine.

7
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:12,000
The strongest videos were not one clever prompt. They came from a kit of eight pieces.

8
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:22,500
A production brief. Visual references. Storyboards written as states. Spring-based motion with synthesized audio.

9
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:34,500
The model cannot watch its film, so it reads a contact sheet, a grid of frames, scores it, and fixes the worst three issues. Then renders again.

10
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:45,000
What the viral runs shared, the post says: long briefs, high effort settings instead of the default, and overnight runs.

11
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:56,000
This video was rendered exactly this way: a seek function, a headless browser, thirty frames per second, ffmpeg. The program is in the repo.

12
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:04,000
The video is a program. Read the article to see every piece.
